# Adding Thumbnails to Media Mentions

This guide explains how to add or update thumbnails for media mentions on the website.

## Media Mentions Structure

Media mentions are stored in `_data/media_mentions.yml` with the following structure:

```yaml
- title: "Article Title"
  url: https://example.com/article
  publisher: Publisher Name
  date: YYYY-MM-DD
  type: Article/Video/Conference Talk/etc.
  language: en/ru
  thumbnail_url: /data/articles_thumbnails/filename.webp
  website_logo_url: /data/webstie_logos/logo.png
```

## Image Requirements

- **Location**: All thumbnails should be stored in `data/articles_thumbnails/`
- **Format**: WebP format preferred for best compression/quality balance
- **Dimensions**: 520×297 pixels (16:9 aspect ratio)
- **Naming Convention**: Use `publisher-YYYY-MM-DD.webp` format
- **Quality**: Use 100% quality for best appearance

## Step-by-Step Process

### 1. Check the existing thumbnails

```bash
ls -la data/articles_thumbnails/
```

### 2. Analyze your source image

```bash
identify -format "%wx%h" source-image.png
```

This will show you the dimensions of your source image.

### 3. Process the image

For best results, use ImageMagick with these options:

```bash
magick source-image.png -resize 520x297^ -gravity center -extent 520x297 -quality 100 data/articles_thumbnails/publisher-YYYY-MM-DD.webp
```

This command:
- Resizes the image to fill the 520×297 frame (`^` operator)
- Centers the content (`-gravity center`)
- Crops to exact 520×297 dimensions (`-extent`)
- Sets maximum quality (`-quality 100`)
- Saves in WebP format

### 4. Verify the processed image

```bash
identify -verbose data/articles_thumbnails/publisher-YYYY-MM-DD.webp | grep Quality
ls -la data/articles_thumbnails/publisher-YYYY-MM-DD.webp
```

### 5. Update the YAML file

Edit `_data/media_mentions.yml` to add the `thumbnail_url` property for your media mention:

```yaml
- title: "Your Article Title"
  # ... other properties ...
  thumbnail_url: /data/articles_thumbnails/publisher-YYYY-MM-DD.webp
```

## Troubleshooting

### Invalid Aspect Ratio

If your source image doesn't match the 16:9 aspect ratio:

1. Option 1: Crop the image manually before processing
2. Option 2: Use the method above with `-resize WIDTHxHEIGHT^` and `-extent WIDTHxHEIGHT` which will automatically crop to fill the frame

### Image Too Small

If your source image is smaller than 520×297:
- Upscaling will reduce quality
- Consider using `-resize` without the `^` operator to maintain aspect ratio and add padding if needed

### File Size Optimization

If the WebP file is too large:
- Reduce quality to 90-95 for minimal visual difference: `-quality 90`
- Consider additional optimizations like: `-define webp:lossless=false -define webp:method=6`

## Example Workflow

```bash
# Check source image
identify -format "%wx%h" /Users/kirill/Downloads/source-image.png
# 1252x710

# Process image to correct dimensions with high quality
magick /Users/kirill/Downloads/source-image.png -resize 520x297^ -gravity center -extent 520x297 -quality 100 data/articles_thumbnails/publisher-2023-01-01.webp

# Verify the result
ls -la data/articles_thumbnails/publisher-2023-01-01.webp
identify -verbose data/articles_thumbnails/publisher-2023-01-01.webp | grep Quality

# Update the YAML file with the new thumbnail reference
# Edit _data/media_mentions.yml
``` 